#version 450
//! `S[h][j] = scale * sum_d Q[h][d] * Kcache[j][h][d]`, attention scores for **one** query.
//!
//! # Why a second scores shader
//!
//! `attn_scores.comp` reads K as `[d_model, 1, T]`, the layout every projection in this runtime
//! writes. That layout is right everywhere it is used — and wrong for a decoder's KV cache, for one
//! reason: appending a position to `[d_model, 1, T]` means writing one element into each of
//! `d_model` runs that are `T` apart. At `d_model` 1024 that is 1024 non-contiguous two-byte
//! copies per tensor per layer per step, which for SMaLL-100's three layers and 128 tokens is over
//! three quarters of a million `vkCmdCopyBuffer` calls.
//!
//! So a cache is **position-major**, `[T, 1, d_model]`, where appending a position is one
//! contiguous copy of `d_model` elements. That is the only difference between this and
//! `attn_scores.comp`, and it is why the existing shader is left alone: `attn_scores.comp`,
//! `softmax.comp` and `attn_apply.comp` are shared by PP-OCRv5 recognition and both Supertonic
//! encoders, so a change to any of their `Push` fields would break three shipping nets at once.
//! This adds a shader instead and touches none of them.
//!
//! # One query, which is what makes a causal mask unnecessary
//!
//! A decode step has exactly one query — the token just produced — attending over the `j < T`
//! positions before it plus itself. The prefix bound *is* the tensor length, so there is nothing to
//! mask: this runtime has no additive mask and needs none. The output is `[heads, 1, keys]`, so
//! `softmax.comp` normalises it unchanged.
//!
//! # The reduction is contiguous here
//!
//! `Kcache[j]` is a run of `d_model` elements and a head owns `head_dim` consecutive channels
//! inside it, so the dot product walks a contiguous vector rather than `head_dim` channels a
//! stride apart. That is strictly better than `attn_scores.comp`'s access pattern; the cost of the
//! layout is paid in `attn_apply_cached.comp`, where V's read becomes the strided one.
//!
//! # Push fields
//!
//! `p.in0` is Q at `[d_model, 1, 1]`, so channel `c` is simply element `c`. `p.in1` is the cache.
//! `p.in_c` is `d_model` and doubles as the cache's per-position stride, `p.group` is the head
//! count, `p.out_w` is the key stride and `p.param0_bits` is `1 / sqrt(head_dim)` as raw f32 bits.
//!
//! When `p.dyn_keys` is set the plan was built once at a maximum length and the step supplies the
//! real one, so `p.out_w` is the *stride* between rows of the score map while the attended count
//! is `step_params.prefix + 1`. Entries past that are left untouched rather than written with a
//! sentinel: `softmax_prefix.comp` reads only the attended span, so what is beyond it never
//! reaches the result.

#include "common.glsl"

void main() {
    uint index = global_index();
    if (index >= p.count) {
        return;
    }
    // The output is `[heads, 1, key_stride]`, so `unpack` gives head and key directly.
    uint head, unused_row, key;
    unpack(index, head, unused_row, key);
    // Outside the range this step attends over. Only possible when the plan is built at a
    // maximum, in which case the dispatch covers the whole stride.
    if (key < attn_first() || key > attn_last(p.out_w)) {
        return;
    }
    uint head_dim = p.in_c / p.group;
    // Q is one position, so its channels are consecutive from `p.in0`.
    uint query = p.in0 + head * head_dim;
    // Position `key` of the cache, then this head's *key/value* head slice within it. Under
    // multi-query attention several query heads land on the same slice.
    uint keys = p.in1 + key * kv_stride(head_dim) + kv_head_of(head) * head_dim;

    float total = 0.0;
    for (uint d = 0u; d < head_dim; d++) {
        total += float(arena[query + d]) * float(arena[keys + d]);
    }
    arena[p.out_at + index] = float16_t(total * uintBitsToFloat(p.param0_bits));
}
